#Git Workflow Research

1. Repository
A repository is a storage location where a project's source code, files, and Git history are maintained.
2. Branch
A branch is a separate line of development. It allows developers to work on features or changes without directly modifying the main code.
Common branches:
•	main – stable production-ready code
•	develop – integration branch for development
•	feature/* – used to develop individual features
3. Commit
A commit is a saved snapshot of changes made to the project.
Example:
git add .
git commit -m "Add employee module"
4. Pull Request
A Pull Request (PR) is a request to merge chang

es from one branch into another branch.
For example:
feature/employee-module → develop
A Pull Request allows other developers to review the code before it is merged.
5. Merge
Merge means combining the changes from one branch into another branch.
Example:
feature/employee-module → develop
After the feature is reviewed and approved, it can be merged into the develop branch.
6. Merge Conflict
A merge conflict happens when Git cannot automatically combine changes from different branches.
For example, if two developers modify the same part of the same file differently, Git may report a conflict.
The developer must review the conflicting changes, choose the correct code, and commit the resolved changes.
7. .gitignore
.gitignore is a file that tells Git which files or folders should not be tracked.
For a Java/Spring Boot project, examples may include:
target/
.idea/
*.class
.env
These files generally should not be committed to the repository.
8. README
README.md is a documentation file that explains the project.
It can contain:
•	Project description
•	Technologies used
•	Setup instructions
•	How to run the application
•	Project structure
•	API information
9. Basic Git Workflow
A common development workflow is:
main
 |
 +--- develop
        |
        +--- feature/employee-module
Workflow Steps
1.	main contains stable production-ready code.
2.	Developers work mainly from the develop branch.
3.	A developer creates a feature branch from develop.
4.	The developer implements the feature.
5.	Changes are committed to the feature branch.
6.	The feature branch is pushed to the remote repository.
7.	A Pull Request is created from the feature branch to develop.
8.	Other developers review the code.
9.	After approval, the Pull Request is merged into develop.
10.	After testing and release preparation, changes can be merged into main.


10. Example Commands
git clone <repository-url>

git checkout develop

git checkout -b feature/employee-module